    "['set forwarding-options port-mirroring input [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig1/0/0 ingress', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig1/0/0 ingress', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the port mirroring (SPAN) commands:\\n\\n##\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig1/0/0 ingress\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig2/0/0 ingress\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig3/0/0 ingress\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, \"monitor session\" is equivalent to Juniper\\'s \"port-mirroring\". The \"ingress\" keyword is used similarly to specify incoming traffic. Cisco typically requires all mirrored sources to be part of the same session (session 1 in this case), whereas Juniper allows separate statements.', 'trans_template': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig1/0/0 ingress'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig2/0/0 ingress', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig2/0/0 ingress', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the port mirroring (SPAN) commands:\\n\\n##\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig1/0/0 ingress\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig2/0/0 ingress\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig3/0/0 ingress\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, \"monitor session\" is equivalent to Juniper\\'s \"port-mirroring\". The \"ingress\" keyword is used similarly to specify incoming traffic. Cisco typically requires all mirrored sources to be part of the same session (session 1 in this case), whereas Juniper allows separate statements.', 'trans_template': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig2/0/0 ingress'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig3/0/0 ingress', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig3/0/0 ingress', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the port mirroring (SPAN) commands:\\n\\n##\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig1/0/0 ingress\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig2/0/0 ingress\\nmonitor session 1 source interface gig3/0/0 ingress\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, \"monitor session\" is equivalent to Juniper\\'s \"port-mirroring\". The \"ingress\" keyword is used similarly to specify incoming traffic. Cisco typically requires all mirrored sources to be part of the same session (session 1 in this case), whereas Juniper allows separate statements.', 'trans_template': 'monitor session 1 source interface gig3/0/0 ingress'}]]": 1,
